p when you're full  because your body trusts that whenever  you're hungry again  you're gonna eat again that is just data  to help you make decisions in the future  about when it feels best to start eating  and later stop now I want to make  something very clear you do not have to  leave food on your plate if you're still  hungry it by all means of course eat  until you're satisfied clear your plate  add more to your plate the idea here is  that you're listening to your body not  the appearance of the food on your plate  although you now know how to identify  fullness you may still be wondering what  if I don't notice these signs and truth  be told that is totally normal  a lot of people have lost the art of  this from years and years of dieting but  the key here is to learn how to increase  your eating consciousness with the  following very important steps number  one limit distractions I'm a mom  multitasking is legitimately my middle  name but parents are otherwise you know  we live in a society where we are  praised for being able to do a lot in a  little amount of time we breakfast while  watching the news we work at our desk  while eating lunch and despite the  notion that we can multitask we actually  aren't as good as it as we might think  in fact when you multitask research  suggests that you're dividing your  brain's resources and attention  resulting in less efficient work sure  you can do it but not to the same degree  as if you were to do each of those tasks  one by one so if you're trying to become  an intuitive eater you really need to  invest the time and effort into doing it  start by limiting distractions around  you so turn the TV off put your work  away really focus on the act of eating  in a distraction-free environment if you  have different things taking up your  attention your attention is no longer  focused on listening to your body's  hunger and fullness cues this is not  about setting rigid rules or you can  never watch TV and eat food at the same  time but it's a good idea to test this  out to see if you find more pleasure in  eating when you're not so distracted you  might  be surprised how much more flavorful and  enjoyable food is when you've got your  full attention on it  number two reinforce your conscious  decision to stop it's easy to tell  ourselves okay you're done eating it's  another to actually stop eating one  useful trick is to give yourself a  visual cue that you're done eating  remember you're done eating when you're  satisfied not when you feel like you  should be done so for example when  you're done with your meal you can put  your napkin on top of your plate that  simple barrier between you and the food  can actually work wonders it reminds you  that you consciously decided that you're  done and if you're still looking to eat  perhaps take a second to just recheck  your hunger to make sure that you're not  actually still hungry if you don't like  the idea of that you can try putting  your food into leftover containers when  you're done eating that way you know you  already have it prepped for another meal  and it's visually telling you that your  meal is complete  number three defend yourself from  obligatory eating remember that you are  in charge of the type of food and how  much food you eat if you're not hungry  you don't have to eat this is always the  case when visiting a relative's house  there is always one food pusher who  wants you to eat and eat and eat I mean  we talked all about this in my holiday  food and body shaming video here so you  can check that out but like I mentioned  in the video although their intentions  are usually good you have the right to  say no it's a good thing to just  remember that you do not need to eat  just for the sake of being polite  finally let's address a really common  problem that a lot of people on the  intuitive eating journey have which is  feeling hungry all the time it may be  frustrating that you're already hungry  after like two hours of eating you  listen to your hunger and fullness cues  and yet again here you are hungry it's  important to keep in mind that there are  a lot of factors that can affect our  fullness they're both biological and  learned by having a better understanding  of the things that cause us to be hungry  or full you may also have a better time  trusting your body and actually feeling  your fullness here are some factors that  may affect your fullness levels number  one time how much time has passed as the  last time you ate it sounds obvious but  the more frequently you eat the less  hungry you end up being going to long  between your meals can cause your blood  sugar levels to plummet your hunger  hormone skyrocketing and you end up  overeating if you know that your lunch  and dinner are going to be very far  apart this is when you'd want to  incorporate some snacks in between this  allows you to fuel your body and brain  and prevents you from experiencing that  intense hunger and again I know this  will be a question but a be snacking  isn't healthy and this leads me to my  second point types of food despite  popular belief snacks are not inherently  unhealthy but of course we can always  make more satiating nutritious choice so  a snack that is high in healthy fats  protein and fiber will help keep you  full much longer than ones that are just  high in simple sugars simple sugars give  you a quick energy boost which is grain  certain situations like a high intensity  workout but without any fiber protein or  good fats it can often be followed by a  rapid crash in blood sugar levels this  can lead you to feel hungry really soon  after so if you're noticing that you're  hungry after a snack or meal checking to  see what that meal or snack consisted of  ideally you want a meal to have a good  source of protein fiber from some whole  grains or veggies and some healthy fats  I call this the hunger crushing  combination and together these compounds  take longer to digest keeping you  feeling full much longer  number three social influences  interestingly we eat differently  depending on who is around us in fact  research suggests that we tend to eat  more food and for much longer when we  eat in large groups and this makes sense  if we're by ourselves  we usually just eat our food and move on  but remember food is a social activity  so of course we spend time eating and  chatting and doing all those fun things  when we're with our friends however  research suggests that some dieters are  more likely to be influenced by the food  choices of others around them and may  purposefully under eat out of fear being  judged especially in the presence of  model eaters in either situation we tend  to ignore our biological cues for hunger  and full  when in front of other people my  suggestion is to remind yourself that no  one knows your body's needs like you do  if your friend is having a salad or  barely touches their meal it may be it's  because they had a big pancake breakfast  or if another friend is having a burger  and fries or polishes off their plate  and pressures you to do the same you  never know they may be skipped breakfast  and are really hungry for a hearty meal  everyone's needs are different so choose  a meal that satisfies your appetite and  make a point to check in with yourself  throughout the meal use the social  activity as an opportunity to really  slow down between bites to engage in  conversation and to check in with your  hunger and fullness throughout the meal  next let's talk about air food now a lot  of diet foods are full of air I mean no  literally like a rice cake is full of  air hence why I say it's a vessel for  peanut butter in my house but hey that  just means now the reason why these  foods are not necessarily the best  snacks on their own is because they only  temporarily decrease hunger the food  enters your stomach and kind of tricks  you into thinking you're full because it  adds volume but because it's lacking any  real sustenance you'll be really hungry  pretty quickly this also can backfire  because these foods tend to have a  health halo effect you know that they're  low in calories so you can eat a large  portion of them to fill your stomach up  but at the end of the day you could have  become full off of a nutrient dense meal  and stayed fuller a lot longer so a lot  of people for example turn to salads as  a healthy option but these two can be  air food as well so lettuce in a bowl by  itself is by no means a salad so here  are some ways to up the staying power of  your salad first is to add protein so  things like shredded chicken tuna  chickpeas kidney beans lentils and tofu  are all great options also add some  carbs quinoa couscous or have some  whole-grain crackers or bread on the  side don't forget about that as well so  olive oil avocado cheese nuts seeds etc  now whether we're talking a salad or  another meal or snack the goal here is  to incorporate slow-digesting  macronutrients so fat protein and fiber  rich carbs again we will talk way more  about this in the  so done gentle nutrition but bottom line  becoming aware of fullness factors can  really help your inner food  anthropologist determine the reasons for  your quickly returning hunger once  you've determined the cause you can  slowly incorporate some intuitive eating  principles to work towards feeling your  fullness for much longer if you feel  like you can't stop eating even if  you're full then this may be a sign that  you're using food as a coping mechanism  Iommi diving into this a lot more when  we discuss principle number seven so  definitely stay tuned for that now your  homework for this week so this week I  really want you to focus on eating  consciously like I mentioned earlier I  want you to take a pause a few times  while you're eating really focus on the  flavors of the food your hunger and  fullness levels and take charge in  deciding when you're done eating find  yourself a visual cue that you can use  to help signal to yourself that you're  done and be sure to rate your fullness  using that satiety scale again a  reminder that there is no right or wrong  with hunger fullness scale just take  notice of where you land before a meal  during a meal and after a meal jot down  how it makes you feel what foods you ate  and how well they helped your hunger  continue to work on these steps  throughout your intuitive eating journey  and of course stay tuned for our next  video on principle number six  discover the satisfaction factor so  folks that is all for today if you liked  it be sure to give it a thumbs up leave  me a comment below if you have any  questions about intuitive eating  subscribe to the channel and I will see  you next time on Abby's kitchen bye  [Music]
 

